---
<LessonPage page={page}>
<main class="wrap" data-core-duration="45–55 minutes" data-extension-duration="15–20 minutes">
  <p class="kicker"><span class="dot"></span> Vocabulary · Lesson 19</p>
  <h1>Common verbs &amp; <em>adjectives</em></h1>
  <p class="standfirst">Turn familiar words into usable language. Learn light-verb chunks such as <span class="ex">make a decision</span>, ordinary verb patterns such as <span class="ex">listen to an update</span>, and adjective frames such as <span class="ex">an expensive ticket</span>, <span class="ex">two expensive tickets</span>, and <span class="ex">The ticket is too expensive for the stated budget.</span></p>
  <div class="meta-row">
    <span class="tag">45–55 min core</span>
    <span class="tag">make · do · take · have</span>
    <span class="tag">direct object · preposition · adjective frame</span>
    <span class="tag">15–20 min optional listening and retrieval</span>
  </div>

  <div class="lesson-flow" aria-label="Lesson flow">
    <div class="flow-step"><b>Notice</b><span>Find the partners that make each word usable.</span></div>
    <div class="flow-step"><b>Discover</b><span>Separate collocations, object patterns, and adjective frames.</span></div>
    <div class="flow-step"><b>Build</b><span>Control form, meaning, degree, and spoken chunks.</span></div>
    <div class="flow-step"><b>Use</b><span>Brief a fictional community-center team.</span></div>
  </div>

  <p class="note-line">A common word is not automatically an easy word. To use it accurately, learn its partners: <span class="ex">do homework</span>, <span class="ex">listen to an announcement</span>, <span class="ex">call the office</span>, and <span class="ex">an inexpensive ticket</span>. These partnerships are called <span class="ex">collocations</span> and <span class="ex">grammar patterns</span>.</p>

  <h2>Two-minute retrieval</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You retrieved third-person agreement, an object pronoun, and present-simple do support.">
    <h3>Three quick checks from earlier lessons</h3>
    <p class="choice-hint">Use the subject and sentence role. Say each complete sentence aloud.</p>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Earlier lesson choices">
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="writes">writes</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="write">write</button>
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="her">her</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="she">she</button>
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="do">do</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="are">are</button>
    </div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>The fictional coordinator is one person: She <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="writes" data-hint="A third-person singular present-simple verb normally adds -s." data-fix="She writes a message every morning." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> a message every morning.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>Rina is already named, and the coordinator calls Rina: The coordinator calls <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="her" data-hint="After the verb calls, use the object form her." data-fix="The coordinator calls her at noon." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> at noon.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>The subject is they and the main verb is work: They <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="do" data-hint="A present-simple negative with they begins with do not." data-fix="They do not work on Sundays." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> not work on Sundays.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div>
    <p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Meet common action verbs</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p>Do not learn these as isolated labels. Connect each verb to one complete example chunk.</p>
  <div class="figure-grid">
    <div class="figure"><span class="fig"><img src="/assets/lessons/img/noto/running.svg" alt="" width="62" height="62" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></span><span class="nm">run in the park</span></div>
    <div class="figure"><span class="fig"><img src="/assets/lessons/img/noto/walking.svg" alt="" width="62" height="62" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></span><span class="nm">walk to the office</span></div>
    <div class="figure"><span class="fig"><img src="/assets/lessons/img/noto/writing.svg" alt="" width="62" height="62" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></span><span class="nm">write a message</span></div>
    <div class="figure"><span class="fig"><img src="/assets/lessons/img/noto/speaking.svg" alt="" width="62" height="62" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></span><span class="nm">speak English</span></div>
    <div class="figure"><span class="fig"><img src="/assets/lessons/img/noto/book.svg" alt="" width="62" height="62" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></span><span class="nm">read a report</span></div>
    <div class="figure"><span class="fig"><img src="/assets/lessons/img/noto/sleeping.svg" alt="" width="62" height="62" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></span><span class="nm">sleep for eight hours</span></div>
    <div class="figure"><span class="fig"><img src="/assets/lessons/img/noto/meal.svg" alt="" width="62" height="62" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></span><span class="nm">eat lunch</span></div>
    <div class="figure"><span class="fig"><img src="/assets/lessons/img/noto/soda.svg" alt="" width="62" height="62" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></span><span class="nm">drink water</span></div>
    <div class="figure"><span class="fig"><img src="/assets/lessons/img/noto/eyes.svg" alt="" width="62" height="62" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></span><span class="nm">see a sign</span></div>
    <div class="figure"><span class="fig"><img src="/assets/lessons/img/noto/ear.svg" alt="" width="62" height="62" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></span><span class="nm">listen to an update</span></div>
    <div class="figure"><span class="fig"><img src="/assets/lessons/img/noto/laptop.svg" alt="" width="62" height="62" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></span><span class="nm">work on a project</span></div>
    <div class="figure"><span class="fig"><img src="/assets/lessons/img/noto/shopping-cart.svg" alt="" width="62" height="62" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></span><span class="nm">buy a ticket</span></div>
    <div class="figure"><span class="fig"><img src="/assets/lessons/img/noto/cooking.svg" alt="" width="62" height="62" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></span><span class="nm">cook dinner</span></div>
    <div class="figure"><span class="fig"><img src="/assets/lessons/img/noto/car.svg" alt="" width="62" height="62" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></span><span class="nm">drive a car</span></div>
    <div class="figure"><span class="fig"><img src="/assets/lessons/img/noto/dancer.svg" alt="" width="62" height="62" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></span><span class="nm">dance at an event</span></div>
    <div class="figure"><span class="fig"><img src="/assets/lessons/img/noto/microphone.svg" alt="" width="62" height="62" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></span><span class="nm">sing a song</span></div>
    <div class="figure"><span class="fig"><img src="/assets/lessons/img/noto/thought-balloon.svg" alt="" width="62" height="62" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></span><span class="nm">think about a problem</span></div>
    <div class="figure"><span class="fig"><img src="/assets/lessons/img/noto/red-heart.svg" alt="" width="62" height="62" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></span><span class="nm">love music</span></div>
    <div class="figure"><span class="fig"><img src="/assets/lessons/img/noto/telephone-receiver.svg" alt="" width="62" height="62" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></span><span class="nm">call the office</span></div>
  </div>

  <h2>Notice the whole light-verb chunk</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="thinking-card">
    <h3>A fictional center opens for the day</h3>
    <p><span class="ex">Rina makes the schedule and makes coffee for the welcome table. Lee does the dishes, then takes a break. At ten, they have a meeting. After the meeting, they have coffee with a visitor.</span></p>
    <ul>
      <li>Which nouns travel with <span class="ex">make, do, take</span>, and <span class="ex">have</span>?</li>
      <li>Why does <span class="ex">make coffee</span> focus on preparing the drink, while <span class="ex">have coffee</span> focuses on drinking it or spending time together?</li>
      <li>Can a rough meaning rule help? When do you still need to remember the complete chunk?</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You selected six established light-verb chunks, including two different viewpoints on coffee.">
    <h3>Discovery check: choose the complete English collocation</h3>
    <p class="choice-hint">The context and noun together select the light verb.</p>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Light verb choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="make">make</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="do">do</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="take">take</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="have">have</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>The team must choose between two schedules today: They need to <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="make" data-hint="English uses make a decision for the completed choice." data-fix="They need to make a decision today." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> a decision today.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>The event kitchen has dirty plates: Lee will <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="do" data-hint="The fixed chore chunk is do the dishes." data-fix="Lee will do the dishes." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> the dishes.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>After ninety minutes of work, the plan gives everyone ten minutes to stop: They <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="take" data-hint="A short planned stop uses take a break in U.S. English." data-fix="They take a ten-minute break." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> a ten-minute break.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>The calendar shows a team discussion at ten: They <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="have" data-hint="A scheduled discussion commonly uses have a meeting." data-fix="They have a meeting at ten." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> a meeting at ten.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span>Rina prepares the drink for the welcome table: She will <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="make" data-hint="Preparing coffee uses make coffee." data-fix="She will make coffee for the welcome table." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> coffee for the welcome table.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">6</span><span>Afterward, Rina sits down and drinks coffee with the visitor: They <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="have" data-hint="Drinking coffee or spending time over coffee uses have coffee." data-fix="They have coffee together." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> coffee together.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div>
    <p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Get to the root: light verbs carry the grammar</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p><span class="ex">Make, do, take</span>, and <span class="ex">have</span> are called light verbs in these chunks. The noun carries much of the specific meaning, but the verb carries tense, agreement, questions, and negatives.</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>light verb</th><th>useful U.S. English chunks</th><th>rough tendency, not a law</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><b>make</b></td><td><span class="ex">make a plan · make a decision · make a mistake · make dinner</span></td><td>create, prepare, or produce a result</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>do</b></td><td><span class="ex">do homework · do the dishes · do a task · do some work</span></td><td>perform work, a task, or an activity</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>take</b></td><td><span class="ex">take a break · take the bus · take a photo · take a shower</span></td><td>several learned event and transport chunks</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>have</b></td><td><span class="ex">have lunch · have a meeting · have a question · have a good time</span></td><td>experience, participate in, eat, drink, or possess</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p class="note-line">The tendencies can help memory, but they cannot generate every answer. Learn the whole chunk. For example, a decision is a result, so <span class="ex">make a decision</span> fits the tendency. English also has the single verb <span class="ex">decide</span>.</p>

  <div class="note">
    <h3>Useful U.S. boundaries</h3>
    <p><span class="ex">Take the bus</span> and <span class="ex">ride the bus</span> are both natural in U.S. English. <span class="ex">Go by bus</span> uses <span class="ex">by</span>. The grammar viewpoint changes, but the transport can be the same.</p>
    <p><span class="ex">Take a shower</span> is especially common in U.S. English. <span class="ex">Have a shower</span> is common in British English and is understood in the United States.</p>
    <p>Neutral U.S. English usually says <span class="ex">exercise</span>, <span class="ex">work out</span>, <span class="ex">get some exercise</span>, <span class="ex">do a workout</span>, or <span class="ex">do some exercises</span>. Avoid using bare <span class="ex">do exercise</span> as a universal chunk.</p>
    <p><span class="ex">Have lunch</span> and <span class="ex">eat lunch</span> are both natural. <span class="ex">Have</span> presents the meal as an event; <span class="ex">eat</span> focuses more directly on consuming food.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>Ordinary verbs need the right complement</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p>Some verbs can stand alone. Others take a direct object or a preposition before their partner.</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>pattern</th><th>examples</th><th>what to store</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>verb can stand alone</td><td><span class="ex">The visitor sleeps. The group walks.</span></td><td>the verb may be complete in context</td></tr>
      <tr><td>verb + direct object</td><td><span class="ex">read a report · write a message · buy a ticket · call the office</span></td><td>no extra preposition before the object</td></tr>
      <tr><td>verb + preposition + partner</td><td><span class="ex">listen to an update · think about a problem · work on a project</span></td><td>store the preposition with the verb</td></tr>
      <tr><td>meaning changes the pattern</td><td><span class="ex">speak English · speak to the coordinator</span></td><td>language is direct; a person commonly follows to or with</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <div class="note">
    <h3>Small words can carry important meaning</h3>
    <p><span class="ex">Listen to</span> means direct attention to sound. <span class="ex">Hear</span> means sound reaches the ears, with or without planned attention. Say <span class="ex">listen to the announcement</span>, not <span class="ex">listen the announcement</span>.</p>
    <p>In ordinary U.S. phone language, say <span class="ex">call the office</span> or <span class="ex">call her</span>, not <span class="ex">call to the office</span> when <span class="ex">call</span> means telephone. The noun frame <span class="ex">make a call</span> is also possible.</p>
    <p>Use <span class="ex">buy + thing</span> but <span class="ex">pay for + thing</span>: <span class="ex">buy a ticket</span>, <span class="ex">pay for the ticket</span>.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You supplied direct objects or required prepositions for six high-frequency ordinary verbs.">
    <h3>Drill 1: complete the ordinary verb pattern</h3>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Ordinary verb partner choices">
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="a report">a report</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="to the announcement">to the announcement</button>
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="English">English</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="to the coordinator">to the coordinator</button>
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="about the problem">about the problem</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="the office">the office</button>
    </div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>The briefing is written, and Rina reads <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="a report" data-hint="Read takes the written thing as a direct object." data-fix="Rina reads a report." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>The audio message begins, and the group listens <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="to the announcement" data-hint="Listen requires to before the sound source." data-fix="The group listens to the announcement." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>The profile names the language Rina uses: She speaks <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="English" data-hint="A language follows speak directly, with no to." data-fix="She speaks English." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>Rina needs a conversation with one person: She speaks <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="to the coordinator" data-hint="A person commonly follows speak to or speak with." data-fix="She speaks to the coordinator." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span>The team considers one problem before deciding: They think <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="about the problem" data-hint="The idea considered follows think about." data-fix="They think about the problem." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">6</span><span>Rina telephones the center: She calls <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="the office" data-hint="Telephone call takes the person or place directly in ordinary U.S. English." data-fix="She calls the office." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div>
    <p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Keep the present-simple form around the chunk</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>purpose</th><th>I / you / we / they</th><th>he / she / it</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>positive</td><td><span class="ex">They make dinner.</span></td><td><span class="ex">She makes dinner.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>negative</td><td><span class="ex">They don’t make dinner.</span></td><td><span class="ex">She doesn’t make dinner.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>question</td><td><span class="ex">Do they make dinner?</span></td><td><span class="ex">Does she make dinner?</span></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p class="note-line">Only one verb carries the third-person marker. In a positive sentence, use <span class="ex">makes, does, takes</span>, or <span class="ex">has</span>. After <span class="ex">does</span> or <span class="ex">doesn’t</span>, return the main verb to its base form: <span class="ex">Does she make...?</span>, not <span class="ex">Does she makes...?</span></p>

  <h2>Adjectives need a position and a viewpoint</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p>An adjective can come before a noun or after <span class="ex">be</span>. It does not change for plural number or gender.</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>frame</th><th>one</th><th>more than one</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>before the noun</td><td><span class="ex">an expensive ticket</span></td><td><span class="ex">two expensive tickets</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>after be</td><td><span class="ex">The ticket is expensive.</span></td><td><span class="ex">The tickets are expensive.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>question with be</td><td><span class="ex">Is the ticket expensive?</span></td><td><span class="ex">Are the tickets expensive?</span></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>Do not add plural <span class="ex">-s</span> to the adjective: <span class="ex">two expensive tickets</span>, not <span class="ex">two expensives tickets</span>. Do not add <span class="ex">do</span> to a simple <span class="ex">be + adjective</span> question: <span class="ex">Is it difficult?</span>, not <span class="ex">Does it difficult?</span></p>

  <div class="note">
    <h3>Common adjective meanings are not automatic labels</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><span class="ex">Easy / difficult</span> depends on the task and person. A task can be easy for one learner and difficult for another.</li>
      <li><span class="ex">Inexpensive / expensive</span> compares price with a context. <span class="ex">Cheap</span> means low-priced but can also suggest low quality. <span class="ex">Inexpensive</span> is the more neutral price word.</li>
      <li><span class="ex">Busy / free</span> can contrast occupied time with available time. <span class="ex">Free</span> also means no cost: <span class="ex">The workshop is free</span>.</li>
      <li><span class="ex">Interesting / boring</span> evaluates a thing or experience. Different people can evaluate it differently.</li>
      <li><span class="ex">Clear / confusing</span> describes information. It does not label the learner’s intelligence or ability.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Many adjective pairs form a useful scale, not a perfect logical switch. <span class="ex">Not expensive</span> does not always mean <span class="ex">cheap</span>, and a person can be both happy and tired. Do not force every adjective into one opposite pair.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="note">
    <h3>Thing or person: boring / bored</h3>
    <p><span class="ex">The talk is boring</span> means the talk causes boredom. <span class="ex">Maya is bored</span> means Maya experiences the feeling. The same distinction appears in <span class="ex">interesting / interested</span> and <span class="ex">confusing / confused</span>.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="note">
    <h3>Very and too are not the same</h3>
    <p><span class="ex">Very</span> increases degree: <span class="ex">The ticket is very expensive.</span> <span class="ex">Too</span> means more than is acceptable or possible for a stated goal: <span class="ex">The budget is $40, but the ticket is $55. It is too expensive for this budget.</span> A high degree alone does not prove <span class="ex">too</span>.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You kept the article, invariant adjective form, be position, and stated-budget meaning of too.">
    <h3>Drill 2: build the adjective frame</h3>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Adjective frame choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="an expensive">an expensive</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="expensive">expensive</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="expensives">expensives</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="too expensive">too expensive</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>The guide names one high-priced option before the singular noun: It is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="an expensive" data-hint="Before one singular ticket, include an because expensive begins with a vowel sound." data-fix="It is an expensive ticket." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> ticket.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>The guide names two high-priced options: They are two <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="expensive" data-hint="An adjective does not add plural -s before a plural noun." data-fix="They are two expensive tickets." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> tickets.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>The adjective follows the singular verb be: The ticket is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="expensive" data-hint="After is, use the unchanged adjective expensive." data-fix="The ticket is expensive." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>The fictional budget is $40, but the ticket costs $55: It is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="too expensive" data-hint="The stated price exceeds the stated budget, so too has a clear limit." data-fix="It is too expensive for the $40 budget." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> for the budget.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div>
    <p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <div class="note">
    <h3>Sound natural: keep the chunk together</h3>
    <p>Light verbs are often less prominent than their noun partner: <span class="ex">make a <strong>DE</strong>cision · do the <strong>DISH</strong>es · take a <strong>BREAK</strong> · have a <strong>MEET</strong>ing</span>. Link the small words: <span class="ex">make-a plan · take-a break · have-a question · listen-to an update</span>.</p>
    <p>Hear the third-person forms clearly. <span class="ex">Makes</span> and <span class="ex">takes</span> end /s/. <span class="ex">Does</span>, <span class="ex">has</span>, <span class="ex">reads</span>, <span class="ex">calls</span>, and <span class="ex">listens</span> end /z/. <span class="ex">Doesn’t</span> is commonly two syllables: <span class="ex"><strong>DUZ</strong>-unt</span>. In relaxed U.S. speech, <span class="ex">Do you...?</span> may link approximately as <span class="ex">d’you</span> or <span class="ex">juh</span>; these are listening guides, not spellings.</p>
    <p>Stress the informative part of an adjective phrase: <span class="ex">an ex-<strong>PEN</strong>-sive <strong>TICK</strong>et · a <strong>DIF</strong>-fi-cult <strong>TASK</strong> · <strong>CLEAR</strong> in-<strong>STRUC</strong>tions</span>. Keep <span class="ex">bored</span> one syllable and <span class="ex"><strong>BOR</strong>-ing</span> two.</p>
  </div>

  <section data-lesson-extension="Tutor-read light-verb viewpoint, free meaning, and thing-person adjective listening check">
