  <h2>Use it safely and flexibly</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p class="note-line">You never need to disclose your real routine, work, study, homework, chores, meals, purchases, budget, transport, home, family, relationships, schedule, opinions, emotions, body, health, abilities, or access needs. Use the fictional center profiles and task cards, invent details, or share personal information only when you freely choose.</p>
  <div class="prompt-card"><b>Complete the task board:</b> Turn supplied nouns into whole chunks with <span class="ex">make, do, take</span>, or <span class="ex">have</span>. Explain when the rough tendency helps and when the chunk must be remembered.</div>
  <div class="prompt-card"><b>Connect ordinary verbs:</b> Use at least one verb that stands alone, two direct-object verbs, and two verb-plus-preposition patterns from the fictional center update.</div>
  <div class="prompt-card"><b>Change present-simple form:</b> Take one routine chunk through a positive sentence, negative sentence, yes-or-no question, and short answer with a third-person subject.</div>
  <div class="prompt-card"><b>Use evidence for adjectives:</b> Describe a fictional ticket, task, workshop, instruction sheet, and talk. State the price, budget, schedule, or profile evidence that makes each adjective defensible.</div>
  <div class="prompt-card"><b>Final production: give a fictional community-center shift briefing for 45–60 seconds.</b> Use one accurate chunk with each of <span class="ex">make, do, take</span>, and <span class="ex">have</span>; two ordinary verb patterns including one preposition; one negative or question with a third-person subject; one adjective before a noun and one after <span class="ex">be</span>; both meanings of <span class="ex">free</span>; one <span class="ex">too</span> judgment based on a stated limit; and one thing/person adjective contrast.</div>

